    "title": "Mark Sanchez Dealt Another Massive Update In His Criminal Trial - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Orlando Silva ,\n1 hours ago\nMark Sanchez. Credit: Ron Chenoy-USA TODAY Sports Mark Sanchez prepares to face a trial in a couple of months. The former NFL quarterback put himself in a complex position in October. Cameras caught him wandering around Indianapolis hours before he called a game between the Las Vegas Raiders and the Indianapolis Colts.\nReports said Sanchez attacked and injured a truck driver, while the driver stabbed him in the chest. Sanchez spent time in the hospital while Fox Sports moved on from him.\nPolice arrested him on a felony count of battery and three misdemeanor charges.\nMark Sanchez’s Upcoming Trial Delayed Mark Sanchez (Robert Deutsch-Imagn Images) WRTV reported on Tuesday that the judge delayed Mark Sanchez’s trial for the fourth time . Initially, it would begin on May 28. Now, it is slated for July 13. A pretrial conference was scheduled for July 8.\n“It is the fourth time Judge Osborn has rescheduled the trial at the request of Sanchez’s attorneys,” WRTV’s Michaela Springer wrote. “In November, he agreed to move the trial start date from December to March. In late February, Osborn granted a second continuance, shifting the trial date from March to April. Then, in late March, Osborn agreed to move the trial start from April to May.”\nHe faces six years in prison and a fine of up to $10,000 as the maximum penalties.\nThe post Mark Sanchez Dealt Another Massive Update In His Criminal Trial originally published on Total Pro Sports .",
    "summary": "Credit: Ron Chenoy-USA TODAY Sports Mark Sanchez prepares to face a trial in a couple of months. “It is the fourth time Judge Osborn has rescheduled the trial at the request of Sanchez’s attorneys,” WRTV’s Michaela Springer wrote. In late February, Osborn granted a second continuance, shifting the trial date from March to April. Then, in late March, Osborn agreed to move the trial start from April to May.” He faces six years in prison and a fine of up to $10,000 as the maximum penalties.",
